jobs.description:
Clemson University, Holcombe Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, seeks a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join a team working at the intersection of safe reinforcement learning, distributed control, and cyber-physical systems security.
The postdoctoral fellow will perform research in the laboratory of Dr. Zoleikha Biron in the Holcombe Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, with affiliations to Clemson's energy systems and autonomy research efforts. Dr. Zoleikha Biron's research program focuses on federally funded work in certified safety for learning-enabled systems, multi-agent and federated learning for critical infrastructure, and resilient distributed autonomy under adversarial conditions.
Applicants should have experience in reinforcement learning and safe/constrained RL (algorithm development, large-scale training, honest multi-seed evaluation), and familiarity with certificate-based safety methods such as control barrier functions, Lyapunov stability analysis, or runtime shielding. Experience in multi-agent RL (centralized training/decentralized execution), federated learning under non-IID client distributions, physics-informed machine learning, Koopman operator methods, or distribution-free uncertainty quantification will be relevant.
Experience with power systems applications (microgrid dispatch, DER integration, false data injection detection), unmanned aerial or multi-robot platforms, or hardware-in-the-loop validation is desirable. Programming and tooling proficiency (Python, PyTorch or JAX, C/C++, MATLAB/Simulink, ROS, multi-node/multi-GPU training, UNIX/LINUX HPC environments) is expected;
Experience releasing and maintaining open-source research software is a plus.
Candidates are expected to be able to:
Follow protocols to execute existing techniques;
Oversee the execution of assigned experiments, including simulation studies and hardware-in-the-loop validation;
Review, approve, or interpret scientific results;
Present research findings to the PI, at laboratory meetings, and to project sponsors;
Design and maintain shared research infrastructure, including simulation environments, distributed training pipelines, benchmark suites, and reproducibility tooling, and train personnel in their use;
Provide day-to-day research mentorship to graduate and undergraduate students;
Actively participate in the writing of grants, papers, and statements of work, including preliminary results, technical sections, and responses to reviewer feedback.
